***[C]***Here's a Rust function that finds the longest common subsequence (LCS) of two strings using dynamic programming:

```rust
/// Finds the longest common subsequence of two strings.
/// 
/// # Arguments
/// 
/// * `s1` - First string
/// * `s2` - Second string
/// 
/// # Returns
/// 
/// The longest common subsequence as a String
/// 
/// # Examples
/// 
/// ```
/// let s1 = "AGGTAB".to_string();
/// let s2 = "GXTXAYB".to_string();
/// assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence(&s1, &s2), "GTAB");
/// ```
fn longest_common_subsequence(s1: &str, s2: &str) -> String {
    let chars1: Vec<char> = s1.chars().collect();
    let chars2: Vec<char> = s2.chars().collect();
    let m = chars1.len();
    let n = chars2.len();

    // Create a DP table where dp[i][j] represents the length of LCS of 
    // first i characters of s1 and first j characters of s2
    let mut dp = vec![vec![0; n + 1]; m + 1];

    // Fill the DP table
    for i in 1..=m {
        for j in 1..=n {
            if chars1[i-1] == chars2[j-1] {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = std::cmp::max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
            }
        }
    }

    // Now construct the LCS from the DP table
    let mut lcs = Vec::new();
    let (mut i, mut j) = (m, n);

    while i > 0 && j > 0 {
        if chars1[i-1] == chars2[j-1] {
            lcs.push(chars1[i-1]);
            i -= 1;
            j -= 1;
        } else if dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1] {
            i -= 1;
        } else {
            j -= 1;
        }
    }

    // The LCS is constructed in reverse order, so reverse it
    lcs.reverse();

    // Convert the vector of characters back to a string
    lcs.iter().collect::<String>()
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_lcs() {
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("ABC", "ABC"), "ABC");
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("ABC", "DEF"), "");
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("AGGTAB", "GXTXAYB"), "GTAB");
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("XMJYAUZ", "MZJAWXU"), "MJAU");
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("ABCDGH", "AEDFHR"), "ADH");
        assert_eq!(longest_common_subsequence("ABC", "AC"), "AC");
    }
}
```

This implementation:

1. Converts the input strings to vectors of characters for easier indexing
2. Creates a dynamic programming table where `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the LCS of the first `i` characters of `s1` and the first `j` characters of `s2`
3. Fills the DP table using the standard LCS recurrence:
   - If characters match: `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1`
   - Otherwise: `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])`
4. Backtracks through the DP table to reconstruct the actual LCS string
5. Returns the LCS as a String

The time complexity is O(m*n) where m and n are the lengths of the two strings, and the space complexity is also O(m*n) for the DP table.

The function includes unit tests to verify its correctness with various test cases.***[C]***

